@@s009 Indexing and slicing NumPy arrays
@@s010 How to read <code>A[:2, 1]</code>: first the rows, then the columns, and the end of the interval is never included. Here the shape of an array, the comma, the slices, the cell of Task 4, the shape of the result and the method for Task 5 of <i>Practical introduction to Python and Numpy</i>, week 1.
@@s011 NumPy arrays: shape and indices from 0
@@s012 NumPy (imported with <code>import numpy as np</code>) is the library for calculations with vectors and matrices. <code>np.array</code> turns a Python list into an <b>array</b>: a list of numbers becomes a vector, a list of lists becomes a matrix, one row for each inner list.
@@s013 Every array has a <b>shape</b> (<code>.shape</code>): a tuple with the length of each dimension, first the rows then the columns. Here <code>A.shape</code> is <code>(3, 3)</code>. The number of dimensions is read with <code>.ndim</code>: 1 for a vector, 2 for a matrix.
@@s014 One counts from zero
@@s015 The "second row" is the index <b>1</b>. The "first column" is the index <b>0</b>. The last element of a row of length 3 has index 2, or −1.
@@s016 Shape, rows and columns of A
@@s017 One element: A[row, column]
@@s018 To read an element one writes the indices in square brackets, separated by a comma: <b>first the row, then the column</b>. <code>A[1, 2]</code> is the element in row 1 and column 2, that is 9.
@@s019 Symbol
@@s020 Meaning
@@s021 take from <code>A</code>
@@s022 separates <b>row</b> (first) and <b>column</b> (after): <code>A[row, column]</code>
@@s023 the last, the penultimate: one counts from the end
@@s024 With <b>a single</b> index, <code>A[0]</code>, NumPy does not take an element but the <b>whole row</b>: it is like writing <code>A[0, :]</code>. It is a classic source of errors, because it looks like an element and is instead a vector.
@@s025 First the row, then the column
@@s026 Slice: start:end, with the end excluded
@@s027 A <b>slice</b> takes an interval of indices with the notation <code>start:end</code>. The rule to remember: <b>the start is included, the end is excluded</b>. <code>v[1:4]</code> takes the indices 1, 2 and 3.
@@s028 Written
@@s029 Meaning
@@s030 <code>start:end</code>
@@s031 from start (included) to end (excluded)
@@s032 from the beginning up to 2 excluded: indices 0 and 1
@@s033 from 3 to the end
@@s034 <code>:</code> alone
@@s035 everything
@@s036 everything, with step 2 (one yes and one no)
@@s037 Why the end is excluded: the number of elements taken is simply <code>end − start</code>, and <code>v[:k]</code> and <code>v[k:]</code> fit together without gaps or duplicates. On a matrix one puts a slice for each dimension, separated by the comma.
@@s038 Five slices on a vector of 6 elements
@@s039 The cell of Task 4, row by row
@@s040 Task 4 asks only to <b>read</b> the cell and understand what it prints: no code is written. Each row uses one of the rules above.
@@s041 Task 4: array indexing
@@s042 A single index on a matrix takes the <b>whole row</b>: <code>[1 2 3]</code>. The name <code>single</code> is misleading: it is not an element.
@@s043 Rows <code>:2</code> = 0 and 1. Column <code>1</code>. At the intersection: <code>A[0,1] = 2</code> and <code>A[1,1] = 4</code> &rarr; <code>[2 4]</code>.
@@s044 All the rows, columns 0 and 1: the first two columns, a 3&times;2 matrix.
@@s045 Last row, last column &rarr; <code>3</code>.
@@s046 Rows from 0 up to the penultimate <b>excluded</b>: with 3 rows only row 0 remains. Columns from 0 up to the last excluded: 0 and 1. Result <code>[[1 2]]</code>, shape <code>(1, 2)</code>.
@@s047 A[:2, 1]
@@s048 dato:chosen rows,due:chosen column,ok:result
@@s049 Before the comma there are the rows. <code>:2</code> means from 0 up to 2, 2 excluded: rows 0 and 1.
@@s050 After the comma the column: a bare number, column 1.
@@s051 A[0,1] = 2 &nbsp;&nbsp; A[1,1] = 4
@@s052 Take the elements at the intersection of each chosen row with the chosen column.
@@s053 The column was a bare number, so that dimension disappears: the result is a vector.
@@s054 The five rows of Task 4 on the matrix
@@s055 Bare number or slice: the shape of the result
@@s056 The same elements can be taken in two ways, and the result has a <b>different shape</b>. A bare number in place of a dimension <b>removes</b> it; a slice, even of length 1, <b>keeps</b> it.
@@s057 Double brackets
@@s058 <code>A[0:1, :]</code> is still a 1&times;3 matrix, and is printed with double brackets <code>[[1 2 3]]</code>. <code>A[0, :]</code> is a vector of 3 elements, <code>[1 2 3]</code>.
@@s059 Why it matters: a matrix <code>(3, 1)</code> and a vector <code>(3,)</code> contain the same numbers, but in products and in element-by-element operations they behave differently (<i>broadcasting</i> can turn a sum into a 3 × 3 matrix). The complete trap is in <a href="nozioni-numpy.html">NumPy: shapes, axes, errors</a>.
@@s060 Doubt asked in chat: is the T a horizontal or vertical view?
@@s061 Neither one nor the other: it is not a <i>view</i>. <code>.T</code> swaps rows and columns and <b>changes the shape</b>: <code>(2, 3)</code> becomes <code>(3, 2)</code>. On a 1D array instead nothing changes, and NumPy gives no error. In full: <a href="w03-matrici.html">Matrices</a> and <a href="nozioni-numpy.html">NumPy</a>.
@@s062 Same numbers, different shapes
@@s063 Translating a request into indices (Task 5)
@@s064 Task 5 asks the opposite of Task 4: from a sentence ("the top right corner", "the second row") write the index. The method is always the same, two questions: <b>which rows?</b> and <b>which columns?</b> Each becomes a bare number (a single row or column, dimension removed) or a slice <code>start:end</code>. Then they are joined with the comma.
@@s065 Two checks before running: the indices start from 0, and the end of the slice is excluded. Afterwards: the <code>.shape</code> of the result must be the one asked for (a 2 × 2 must have shape <code>(2, 2)</code>).
@@s066 Shows the solutions of Task 5
@@s067 Top-right 2 × 2 corner: rows 0 and 1, columns 1 and 2 &rarr; <code>ur = A[:2, 1:]</code> = <code>[[2, 3], [4, 9]]</code>. Second row &rarr; <code>row = A[1]</code> = <code>[3, 4, 9]</code>. First column &rarr; <code>col = A[:, 0]</code> = <code>[1, 3, 5]</code>. Checked with the <code>assert</code> of the notebook.
@@s068 From a sentence to an index
